<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wahran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oran is a port city on the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, founded in 902 by Andalusi sailors. It was a trading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, Spanish from 1509 to 1708 and again from 1732 to 1792, and then the seat of the Ottoman bey of the west. Under French rule from 1831 it became Algeria's second city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oran.jpg" alt="Oran" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R hakka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:31-4_Chapelle_de_Santa-Cruz_(7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tashfin's death near <b>Oran</b> in 1145 opened Morocco to the Almohads, and when Abd al-Mu'min's forces stormed Marrakesh in 1147, killing the last Almoravid ruler, the young Ishaq ibn Ali, and demolishing Almoravid monuments, including the palace on whose site the Koutoubia Mosque would rise, the erasure was…</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1942 Allied forces landed at Algiers and <b>Oran</b> in Operation Torch, and in 1943 Algiers became the seat of the French Committee of National Liberation, headed jointly by Charles de Gaulle and Henri Giraud until de Gaulle took sole control; Algerian units then fought in Italy…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Granada, Spain seized a chain of North African ports, among them Mers el-Kébir in 1505, <b>Oran</b> in 1509 and Bejaia in 1510, and around 1510 it built a fort on the Peñón, an islet commanding the harbour of Algiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mustapha Bouchelaghem captured <b>Oran</b> in 1708, though Spain retook it in 1732.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-the-west">Beylik of the West</a></li><li><blockquote>Its exact borders are unknown, but it was centred on the Tlemcen region and the <b>Oran</b> plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans">Kingdom of the Moors and Romans</a></li><li><blockquote>It lived from maritime trade with North Africa, and its sailors founded trading posts on the African coast, including <b>Oran</b> in 902.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pechina">Republic of Pechina</a></li></ul></section>
